  "meta": {
    "title": "Cold Seep Mud: Methane Hydrate Cracks of the Gulf of Mexico Slope",
    "date": "2026-07-31",
    "lesson": "At Bush Hill and similar Gulf of Mexico cold seeps, methane and hydrogen sulfide leak from cracked hydrate deposits into cold, dark bottom water around 550m depth. Free-living sulfide-oxidizing bacteria (relatives of Beggiatoa and Sulfurovum) don't wait passively for chemical energy - they use chemotaxis to swim up sulfide gradients seeping from the seafloor, competing with mat-forming mussels' symbionts and methanotrophs for the thin, patchy plume. The water column here is a deep, cold, food-poor desert except for these chemical hotspots: light is absent below the photic zone, so the entire community runs on chemosynthesis. This level asks the player to hunt a faint, deep chemical plume in near-total darkness and cold, competing against slow grazers and viral pressure, rather than out-eating a rich food field.",
    "citation": "Cordes et al. 2010, Lloyd et al. 2010 - cold seep chemosynthetic community structure, Gulf of Mexico",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Gulf of Mexico cold seep methanotroph/thiotroph mats (e.g. Bush Hill, GC185), ~550m depth"
  },
